I can do this with a scheduler like quartz, but I want to know if there is a simpler way to do this in spring boot.

Spring Boot provides a feature called `@Scheduled` annotation which can be used to schedule tasks to run at a fixed rate. 

Here is an example of how you can use it:

```java
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.*;

@Component
public class MyScheduler {

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 10000) // run every 10 seconds
    public void runQuery() throws SQLException, IOException {
        // read sql query from file
        String query = readQueryFromFile("path/to/query.sql");

        // connect to database
        Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb", "username", "password");

        // execute query
        Statement statement = connection.createStatement();
        ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery(query);

        // store result in file
        storeResultInFile(resultSet, "path/to/result.csv");

        // close connection
        connection.close();
    }

    private String readQueryFromFile(String filePath) throws IOException {
        File file = new File(filePath);
        return new String(Files.readAllBytes(file.toPath()));
    }

    private void storeResultInFile(ResultSet resultSet, String filePath) throws SQLException, IOException {
        try (FileWriter writer = new FileWriter(filePath)) {
            while (resultSet.next()) {
                writer.write(resultSet.getString(1) + "," + resultSet.getString(2) + "\n");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the `runQuery` method is annotated with `@Scheduled` and will run every 10 seconds. Inside the method, we read the SQL query from a file, execute it against the database, and store the result in another file.

Note that you will need to replace the placeholders in the `DriverManager.getConnection` method with your actual database credentials and connection details.

Also, make sure to add the necessary dependencies to your `pom.xml` file (if you're using Maven) or your `build.gradle` file (if you're using Gradle):

```xml
<!-- Maven -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>mysql</groupId>
    <artifactId>mysql-connector-java</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```groovy
// Gradle
dependencies {
    implementation 'mysql:mysql-connector-java'
}
